there are two research projects that I'm  working on currently which I think are  intriguing and important one of them is  looking at the nature of trying to make  a cohesive citizenship out of diversity  and understanding more some of the  cultural concerns related to that  project and I'm interested both in the  majority native-born population that for  very understandable reasons feels that  their society is changing quite quickly  when you have when you have newcomers  coming from very diverse religious or  ethnic or racial backgrounds and  different cultures and so I'm very  interested in knowing how can you help  create sort of cross group dialogues and  a an easier process of integration for  both of those groups and then also on  the immigrant side how do immigrants and  how can they reimagine their position in  a society where the modal person of that  society might not be someone who looks  like themselves so someone who might be  of a religious minority background but  it's perceived that someone who is  American or Canadian has to be Christian  and so how do you reimagine the national  identity your nationality in a way that  becomes inclusive so there's that  project another project that I'm very  interested in which builds on some of my  previous research is that I think that  civil society and how robust civil  society is for immigrant communities is  fundamental for them to get to make  advances in all kinds of areas so not  just becoming a citizen but helping  people to get into the labor market  helping people who maybe are facing you  know social isolation seniors who might  not have a lot of support networks and  so community-based organizations do a  lot of fundamental work on that and one  of the questions I'm asking in the  American context right now is whether  the incidence of community-based  organization the nonprofit organizations  that do so much important work in our  communities whether they are really  targeting immigrant issues and immigrant  populations to the same extent that we  find immigrants in the general  population our research seems to be  suggesting that the number  of community-based organizations that  deal with immigrant issues is only half  as much as what we would expect given  the immigrants in the population and  this really raises huge questions about  civics civic stratification so  inequality in our community-based  organizations and inequality in the  civil society that sort of helps us get  things done and I'm interested in  knowing why
 

